# nerdy-story — product & MVP constraints

Enforce these decisions. Do not invent product, scope, or stack choices beyond what is listed here.

## Product concept

- **Name:** nerdy-story
- English reading literacy app for kids, narrative-driven, AI-powered
- **Age band:** 7–9 years old / 2nd–3rd grade reading level — all story text, vocab difficulty, and sentence complexity must be calibrated to this band
- **Core loop:** pre-written interactive story with an embedded vocabulary challenge mechanic, live-AI-graded

## Story structure

- Story is **pre-written by the developer**, not generated live (controls reading-level accuracy, removes demo risk)
- **Branching:** 1 diverge point → 2 paths → 2 possible endings
- The choice at the diverge point is **not** comprehension-gated — both paths are equally valid narratively. Purpose: replay/reread incentive ("what if I'd chosen differently?") to support fluency through rereading, not to test comprehension
- **Chapter 2** does not need to exist as playable content — the demo only needs to show the unlock happening, not deliver chapter 2 itself

## Vocabulary challenge mechanic

- 8–10 challenge/"mystery" words embedded in the story (exact count/selection deprioritized until core loop works)
- Mystery words are visually highlighted; the challenge opens as an **overlay/modal** over the story page. After grading/feedback, the overlay closes and the child returns to the **same** story page
- **Challenge format:** "Explain what you understand by [word]" — open-ended; **not** multiple choice; **not** "use it in a sentence"
- Correctness is graded by a **live API call** to **chatgpt-4o-mini** (OpenAI) that evaluates whether the child's explanation semantically matches the word's real meaning
- Grading must be calibrated for a 7–9 year old's language: accept simplified phrasing, synonyms, and partial-but-correct understanding; reject answers describing a different/wrong concept
- Grading prompt must supply the model with the **target definition** and instruct it to return a **structured, parseable result** (not free-form prose) — likely a strict correct/incorrect signal plus a short kid-friendly reason
- **Hints are pre-written, not AI-generated** — 2–3 tiers per word, triggered on a wrong answer (live-generated hints rejected to reduce live-AI surface area)
- **Soft progression:** the child is not hard-blocked forever on a correct answer. After the retry limit, reveal the word's meaning in a kid-friendly way (pre-written, not live-AI), then unlock **Next Page**. Meaning reveal does **not** count as a correct answer for words-learned

## Feedback & progress

- After grading, the child sees **why** an answer was right or wrong (feedback loop), not just correct/incorrect
- Story progression (**Next Page**) stays gated until the challenge is **resolved** — either an accepted explanation, or meaning reveal after the retry limit — then the overlay closes and Next Page becomes available on the same story page
- **Words learned** = count of challenge words answered correctly within the **current session** (session-scoped for the demo; not a multi-day / persisted progress metric)
- Words-learned count increments **live on screen** as a direct result of correct answers only, not on meaning reveal, and not revealed only at the end
- **Ending beat:** book-coloring animation + words-learned count, followed by a chapter-unlock reveal — this is the closing moment of the demo

## Loading / latency

- The **only** live-AI dependency in the whole app is the meaning-grading call
- Latency is absorbed with a minimal, playful loading state (cat/dictionary theme was the working idea) — keep simple; do not over-invest

## Tech stack

- **Runtime:** Bun — officially supported Vercel Functions runtime; no shim/third-party runtime package
- **Language:** TypeScript
- **Framework:** Next.js (App Router) — for API routes (grading API credentials stay server-side, never exposed to the client) and frontend+backend as one deployable unit; **not** chosen for SEO/SSR
- **Grading model:** chatgpt-4o-mini (OpenAI) — the only live-AI dependency; API key stays server-side
- **State:** plain React state/Context — no TanStack Query (no caching/refetching problem); no Zustand for MVP (no cross-cutting client-state complexity yet); revisit only if the app grows post-MVP
- **Styling:** Tailwind
- **Components:** shadcn/ui — owned generated component source (not a black-box import). Radix comes in as a dependency of shadcn; not a separate install decision
- **Animation:** Motion (Framer Motion) — for sequenced/orchestrated closing beat (words-learned count-up → unlock reveal); already chosen
- **Deployment:** Vercel
- **Testing:** Vitest

## Process constraints

- **No human-written code** — 100% AI-authored (Cursor + harness)
- **Deadline:** September 7; deliverable is a 2–3 minute demo video
- **Scope discipline:** build the smallest version of the full loop that demos well; resist adding chapter 2, settings, auth, or persistence for the MVP

## Explicit non-goals (MVP)

- Playable chapter 2 content
- Settings, auth, persistence
- Live-generated hints
- Live-generated story text
- TanStack Query, Zustand (unless post-MVP growth requires them)
- Over-investing in loading UX beyond a minimal playful state
